### Muon-trident rate versus lead thickness with a small bias factor: definition for
### scripts/trident_scan.sh (the script reads these aliases, every process executes this file).
### In the build directory, in the background, at low priority and keeping the computer awake:
###   mkdir -p output/tridents_F1e3
###   nohup nice -n 15 caffeinate -is sh scripts/trident_scan.sh macro/trident_scan_def.mac 10 >> output/tridents_F1e3/scan.log 2>&1 &
### Stop after the running processes: touch output/tridents_F1e3/STOP; go on: delete STOP, start again.
### Each thickness t gives output/tridents_F1e3/t<t>/ with trident_rate.png/.pdf/.txt
### (analysis/trident_rate.C) as soon as its processes are done; output/tridents_F1e3/summary.txt at the end.
###
### F = 1e3 instead of the 1e5 of scripts/trident_cosmic.sh: with 1e5 a muon often has a second biased
### trident, which gets weight 1/F^2, so the rate came out ~5-6% low (20-30% above 100 GeV); with 1e3
### this is ~0.05%. 100 times more muons keep the number of simulated tridents: 80 x 10^7 muons per
### thickness = 519 h of sea-level exposure (8 x 10^6 = 5.19 h before), about 8 h on 10 cores.
### The 2 x 2 m source plane is at z = 72 cm for every thickness (lead top 71 cm at 50 cm, as in
### lead_thick_scan_def.mac); it covers all detectable tridents (>= 99.7% of those of the 42 cm runs).

/control/alias tridentScanDir output/tridents_F1e3
# thicknesses (cm), run in this order
/control/alias tridentScanLead "10 20 30 40 50"
# processes per thickness and muons per process
/control/alias tridentScanJobs 80
/control/alias tridentEvents 10000000
/control/alias tridentBias 1e3
/control/alias tridentPlaneZ 72
# seed of process k at thickness t: tridentScanSeed0 + 1000 t + k
/control/alias tridentScanSeed0 2000000
